 "reconciliation": {
  "old_conflict": "prices.json measured a combined crude+gas index of 3.32 for 2022 from the customs residual; gas_prices.json modelled Turkish Russian gas at 1.75-1.87 with an oil formula. The map skipped the measured value (PRICES_SKIP) and used the oil-formula gas index.",
  "finding": "The oil-formula assumption is wrong from 2022. Gazprom and BOTAS added hub (TTF) indexation to the contracts in 2021, and in 2022-2025 BOTAS bought 4.5-6.3 bcm a year of Russian gas as spot pipeline gas (EPDK). With EPDK volumes and hub-linked prices the gas price is about 100 EUR/MWh in 2022 and 35-38 in 2023-2025, against about 18.7 in 2018-2021 (oil-indexed with the 10.25% BOTAS discount). The contract model and the customs residual agree within 3% in 2022-2023 and within 15% in 2024-2025. The reported BOTAS debt to Gazprom of about USD 20 bn at the end of 2022 (OSW) is only possible with a gas bill near the residual (about USD 23 bn), not with the oil formula (about USD 7 bn).",
  "which_to_trust": "The measured value. The new combined crude+gas index for 2022 is 3.2 (crude 1.41, gas 5.3), close to the old 3.3; fossil_all 2022 is 2.80 against 2.84 in prices.json. gas_prices.json TUR (1.87 in 2022) understates the price by a factor of about 2.8 in 2022 and 1.3-1.6 in 2023-2025."
 },
